        "content": "Now I have enough data to write the comprehensive audit. Let me compile all findings and create the output files.",
        "refusal": null,
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "id": "toolu_01NU9QtYS1Db5inggLUa7kyR",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "bash",
              "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > /home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/audit-report.json << 'AUDITEOF'\\n{\\n  \\\"version\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"lastUpdated\\\": \\\"2026-03-16T07:12:42.000000+00:00\\\",\\n  \\\"overallVerdict\\\": \\\"issues-found\\\",\\n  \\\"perspectives\\\": {\\n    \\\"agentAsFunction\\\": {\\n      \\\"checksRun\\\": 16,\\n      \\\"checksPassing\\\": 10,\\n      \\\"checksFailing\\\": 6,\\n      \\\"findings\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-CONTRACT-02\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"all 8 coordinators with revision-loop\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Result code 'revision-loop' declared in roster but never emitted by any coordinator routing table. Dead result code creates an unreachable orchestrator routing rule.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Roster entries for concept-coordinator, worldbuilding-coordinator, character-coordinator, plotting-coordinator, style-coordinator, drafting-coordinator, revision-coordinator, and beta-reading-coordinator all declare 'revision-loop' in resultCodes. However, none of their routing tables contain any action that writes 'revision-loop' to their own status.json. The orchestrator has a catch-all rule for 'revision-loop' that reads: 'Wait — coordinator is in its internal auditor retry loop'. This rule can never trigger because no coordinator ever writes this result code.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Either (a) remove 'revision-loop' from all coordinator resultCodes and remove the orchestrator's catch-all routing rule, or (b) add an explicit routing action in each coordinator that writes result='revision-loop' as a transient state during auditor retry cycles (e.g., after deleting sub-statuses and before re-dispatching).\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-ARTIFACT-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"all 8 auditors + their parent coordinators\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Audit-report gate files (audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json) are written by auditors but not listed in any coordinator's 'reads' field in the roster, creating an artifact ownership gap in the declared contract.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"The roster shows 8 auditors writing audit-reports/*.json (e.g., concept-auditor writes audit-reports/concept/gate.json). Coordinators reference these files in their routing table actions (e.g., worldbuilding-coordinator action: 'read audit-reports/worldbuilding/gate.json to identify failing area'). However, the coordinators' 'reads' arrays in roster.json do NOT include these audit-report paths. For example, concept-coordinator reads: ['progress.json', 'story-config.json', 'agents/concept-developer/status.json', 'agents/craft-profile-selector/status.json', 'agents/concept-auditor/status.json'] — no audit-reports entry.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add the corresponding audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json to each coordinator's 'reads' array in roster.json: concept-coordinator reads audit-reports/concept/gate.json, worldbuilding-coordinator reads audit-reports/worldbuilding/gate.json, etc. This affects 7 coordinators (concept, worldbuilding, character, plotting, drafting, revision, beta-reading — style-coordinator also references it).\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-MANIFEST-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"21 agents (orchestrator + 20 coordinators/sub-coordinators)\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Coordinators, sub-coordinators, and the orchestrator do not include manifest.json prepend instructions in their prompts. Only 46 of 67 agents reference manifest.json.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"grep -L 'manifest.json' agents/*.agent.md returns 21 agents: the orchestrator, all 9 coordinators, all 10 sub-coordinators, and the guide. While coordinators are pure routers and their status writes are minimal, the manifest is the audit trail — every agent that writes status.json should prepend to manifest.json.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add 'Prepend entry to manifest.json' to the Status Contract section of all coordinator and sub-coordinator prompts. The guide is exempt (no status.json). The orchestrator should also prepend on terminal status writes (delivered, delivered-with-gaps, failed).\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-ROUTE-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"info\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer (orchestrator)\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Orchestrator routing table uses wildcard 'agents/{any-coordinator}/status.json' for the revision-loop rule but uses specific agent paths for all other rules. Inconsistent read-column syntax.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"24 routing rules use specific paths like 'agents/concept-coordinator/status.json', but rule 24 uses the wildcard pattern 'agents/{any-coordinator}/status.json'. The orchestrator's roster reads field is 'agents/*/status.json' (also wildcard). The inconsistency is cosmetic but could confuse implementations that parse routing tables literally.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Normalize: either expand the wildcard rule into per-coordinator rules (more explicit, matches the pattern of all other rules), or document the wildcard convention in the routing design notes.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-ARTIFACT-02\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"chapter-drafter + 3 downstream editors\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"chapters/{N}/draft.md has 4 concurrent writers without explicit read-modify-write ordering protocol in the roster or architecture. The agents run sequentially (chapter-drafter → pov-voice-maintainer → continuity-integrator → craft-enforcer), but this ordering is enforced only by the sub-coordinator routing table, not by the artifact contract itself.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"roster.json shows chapters/{N}/draft.md written by: chapter-drafter, pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer. architecture.json lists an ownership field 'lastUpdatedChapter' for tracking artifacts but chapters/{N}/draft.md is not in the tracking artifacts list — it's a core creative artifact. The creative-writing-coordinator dispatches chapter-drafter then pov-voice-maintainer sequentially, and quality-integration-coordinator dispatches continuity-integrator then craft-enforcer sequentially. The sequential dispatch prevents actual concurrent writes, but the artifact contract doesn't declare this dependency.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add an explicit note in the architecture artifacts section that chapters/{N}/draft.md follows a sequential-writer-chain pattern (not true multi-writer). Document the required write order: drafter → voice → continuity → craft. Alternatively, have downstream agents write to separate review files rather than editing the draft in place.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-ARTIFACT-03\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"chapter-drafter vs. continuity-tracker and craft-tracker\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Cross-cutting tracker artifacts (continuity-tracker.json, foreshadowing-ledger.json, emotional-throughline.json, information-asymmetry-map.json, mystery-box-inventory.json) are written by both the chapter-drafter (inline during drafting) and by dedicated cross-cutting specialists (continuity-tracker, craft-tracker), creating potential write conflicts if dispatch order is not strictly enforced.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"The chapter-drafter's Step 6 says 'Append to continuity-tracker.json: new events...'. The continuity-tracker specialist also writes continuity-tracker.json. The orchestrator dispatches continuity-tracker AFTER drafting-coordinator completes (as a full verification pass). So the chapter-drafter writes tracker updates per-chapter during drafting, and then the continuity-tracker re-processes after all chapters are done. This is semantically a read-modify-write cycle but not declared as such. If the continuity-tracker overwrites rather than merges the chapter-drafter's entries, data loss occurs.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Document the write protocol explicitly: chapter-drafter performs incremental appends during drafting; continuity-tracker and craft-tracker perform authoritative read-verify-update passes afterward. The tracker specialists should be instructed to MERGE with (not overwrite) chapter-drafter entries. Add a 'writeProtocol: append-then-verify' field to these artifacts in architecture.json.\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"fractalWorkflow\\\": {\\n      \\\"checksRun\\\": 16,\\n      \\\"checksPassing\\\": 11,\\n      \\\"checksFailing\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"findings\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-HIERARCHY-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer (orchestrator)\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Orchestrator dispatches 3 specialist agents directly (continuity-tracker, series-kb-manager, craft-tracker), violating the pure hierarchy rule that orchestrators should dispatch only coordinators.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"roster.json shows romantic-fantasy-writer (level=orchestrator) with 12 children: 9 coordinators + 3 specialists (continuity-tracker, series-kb-manager, craft-tracker). The orchestrator prompt's routing table directly dispatches these 3 specialists: 'dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer-craft-tracker (initialize)' after style completes, 'dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer-continuity-tracker (full verification)' after drafting completes, 'dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer-series-kb-manager' after polish completes. These are cross-cutting concerns that don't fit into any single coordinator's domain.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"This is an architectural trade-off, not necessarily a bug. Options: (a) Accept as a documented exception — cross-cutting specialists may be dispatched by the orchestrator when they span multiple coordinator domains; (b) Create a cross-cutting-coordinator to manage all 3 cross-cutting specialists; (c) Move these specialists under their most-relevant coordinator (craft-tracker → style-coordinator, continuity-tracker → revision-coordinator, series-kb-manager → polish-coordinator). Option (a) is pragmatic if documented.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-PURITY-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"info\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer (orchestrator)\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Orchestrator performs substantive routing logic in one rule that goes beyond pure routing: the beta-reading→revision feedback loop contains conditional business logic ('check beta-synthesis verdicts: if any chapter verdict==revision-required AND revisionBetaCycles < maxRevisionBetaCycles → reset revision+beta status, re-dispatch').\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Routing table rule 18: 'check beta-synthesis verdicts: if any chapter verdict==revision-required AND revisionBetaCycles < maxRevisionBetaCycles → reset revision+beta status, re-dispatch revision-coordinator; else update progress.json beta-reading→complete, dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer-polish-coordinator'. This rule reads beta-synthesis verdicts (a domain-specific artifact), interprets chapter-level verdicts, and makes a multi-branch decision. Pure routers should only read status files and dispatch based on result codes.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Extract the beta-synthesis verdict evaluation into a dedicated specialist agent (e.g., 'beta-verdict-evaluator') that reads beta-synthesis data and writes a simple status.json with result='revision-required' or result='approved'. The orchestrator then routes based on that status code rather than interpreting domain artifacts directly.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-CONVERGE-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"info\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"drafting-coordinator, revision-coordinator, beta-reading-coordinator\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Per-chapter iteration loops reference 'maxAuditorRetries' and 'maxRevisionBetaCycles' bounds but these values are defined only in routing.json globalBounds, not embedded in the agent prompts themselves. Agents must know where to read these bounds at runtime.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"routing.json globalBounds: {maxAuditorRetries: 3, maxRevisionBetaCycles: 2}. Coordinator prompts reference these as variables (e.g., 'chapterRetries < maxAuditorRetries') but never specify where the value comes from. The drafting-coordinator prompt contains 'result == failed AND chapterRetries < maxAuditorRetries' but doesn't say maxAuditorRetries=3 or where to read it.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Either embed the bound values directly in each coordinator's prompt (## Configuration section with maxAuditorRetries=3, maxRevisionBetaCycles=2), or specify that these values are read from a runtime config file (e.g., pipeline-config.json) and add that file to coordinators' reads lists.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-LAZY-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"beta-synthesizer\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Beta-synthesizer agent is an aggregation/synthesis agent that produces chapter verdicts driving the revision↔beta feedback loop, but lacks Anti-Laziness Rules despite its gate-keeping function.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"The beta-synthesizer reads 5 beta reader feedback files and writes a synthesis verdict per chapter (beta-synthesis/{N}.json) that determines whether a chapter needs re-revision. This verdict is consumed by the orchestrator's routing rule to decide revision-required vs. approved. A lazy synthesizer that rubber-stamps 'approved' would let quality issues through. However, grep -l 'Anti-Laziness' agents/*.agent.md shows only 10 agents have anti-laziness rules — the 8 auditors, craft-enforcer, and craft-tracker. The beta-synthesizer is not among them.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add Anti-Laziness Rules to the beta-synthesizer prompt requiring: (1) citation of specific findings from each beta lens in every synthesis, (2) minimum observation count before issuing an 'approved' verdict, (3) second-pass requirement if zero issues found across all 5 lenses, (4) explicit statement that its verdicts will be audited by the beta-reading-auditor.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-LAZY-02\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"5 beta reader specialists\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Beta reader specialists (romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality) function as adversarial evaluation agents but none have Anti-Laziness Rules, despite producing feedback that gates chapter quality.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"All 5 beta readers (romantic-fantasy-writer-romance-beta-reader, fantasy-beta-reader, craft-beta-reader, sensitivity-beta-reader, originality-beta-reader) read revised chapters and produce structured feedback that feeds into the beta-synthesizer's verdict. They are adversarial in nature — their job is to find problems. However, none include Anti-Laziness Rules sections in their prompts. Compare with the auditors (concept-auditor, worldbuilding-auditor, etc.) which all have explicit anti-laziness rules with item-by-item verification requirements and zero-findings suspicion clauses.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add Anti-Laziness Rules to all 5 beta reader prompts with lens-specific requirements: minimum finding count per chapter, evidence requirements (quote the passage), second-pass requirement if zero issues found, and a note that their feedback will be verified by the beta-reading-auditor.\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"summary\\\": {\\n    \\\"totalChecks\\\": 32,\\n    \\\"totalPassing\\\": 21,\\n    \\\"totalFailing\\\": 11,\\n    \\\"criticalFindings\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"warningFindings\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"infoFindings\\\": 3,\\n    \\\"newIssues\\\": \\\"8 new warnings and 3 new info findings not caught by checklist-validator. The checklist-validator found 40 warnings (all coordinator missing ask_questions + Key Invariants). This audit found 11 additional issues across artifact contracts, routing dead codes, hierarchy violations, purity leaks, and anti-laziness gaps.\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\nAUDITEOF\\necho \\\"audit-report.json written\\\"\", \"description\": \"Write audit-report.json\", \"initial_wait\": 10}"
            }
