              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory-audit-oracle/output.md\", \"file_text\": \"# Audit Oracle Report — Romantic Fantasy Writer Agent System\\n\\n**Audit Date:** 2026-03-16T07:12:42Z\\n**Domain:** romantic-fantasy-writer (67 agents, depth-3 hierarchy)\\n**Prior Verification:** Checklist-validator scored 1329/1369 (97.1%), 0 critical, 40 warnings\\n**Audit Verdict:** issues-found (0 critical, 8 warnings, 3 info)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n\\nThe romantic-fantasy-writer agent system is architecturally sound. The 67-agent hierarchy correctly implements the fractal pattern: 1 guide → 1 orchestrator → 9 coordinators → 10 sub-coordinators → 47 specialists. Routing tables form a complete DAG with no dead-end chains that lose work. Artifact data flows are acyclic at the phase level. Convergence bounds exist for all iterative loops.\\n\\nHowever, this audit found **11 issues not caught by the checklist-validator** — 8 warnings and 3 info items spanning both the agent-as-function and fractal-workflow perspectives. The most significant patterns:\\n\\n1. **Dead result code (`revision-loop`)** — declared in 8 coordinators but never emitted, creating a phantom routing rule in the orchestrator\\n2. **Artifact ownership gaps** — audit-report gate files used in coordinator routing but not declared in coordinator reads\\n3. **Anti-laziness enforcement gaps** — 6 adversarial agents (beta-synthesizer + 5 beta readers) lack anti-laziness rules\\n4. **Hierarchy exception** — orchestrator dispatches 3 specialists directly (cross-cutting concern trade-off)\\n\\nNone are critical blockers — all are addressable in the gap-hunting pass without architectural changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Perspective 1: Agent-as-Function Audit\\n\\n### Contract Checks\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-CONTRACT-01: Status.json Schema Consistency\\nAll 66 non-guide agents declare consistent status contracts with result, summary, timestamps, and artifactsProduced fields. The guide is explicitly exempted (user-facing, no status.json). **PASS**\\n\\n#### ⚠️ AF-CONTRACT-02: Result Code Validity\\n**FINDING:** 8 coordinators (concept, worldbuilding, character, plotting, style, drafting, revision, beta-reading) declare `revision-loop` as a result code, but no coordinator routing table ever writes `result: 'revision-loop'` to its status.json. The orchestrator has a catch-all rule for `result == 'revision-loop'` that says \\\"Wait — coordinator is in its internal auditor retry loop\\\" — this rule is unreachable.\\n\\n**Evidence:** Checked all 8 coordinator routing tables in routing.json. Each coordinator handles auditor failure by either (a) deleting sub-statuses and re-dispatching (if retries < max), or (b) writing `result: 'blocked'` (if retries >= max). At no point does any coordinator write `result: 'revision-loop'`.\\n\\n**Impact:** Low — the dead rule causes no harm, but it pollutes the routing table and the result code contract. An implementation reading `revision-loop` as a possible state would create unnecessary wait logic.\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-CONTRACT-03: Artifact Path Correspondence\\nAll specialist agents declare artifact writes that correspond to paths they actually produce in their Process steps. Verified across all 47 specialists. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-CONTRACT-04: next_hint Chain Validity\\nNot applicable — this system uses routing tables rather than next_hint dispatch chains. Coordinators dispatch children based on status reads, not hints. **PASS (N/A)**\\n\\n### Routing Integrity\\n\\n#### ⚠️ AF-ROUTE-01: Routing Coverage Completeness\\n**FINDING (minor):** The orchestrator's routing table uses inconsistent read-column syntax — 23 rules use specific paths (`agents/concept-coordinator/status.json`) but rule 24 uses a wildcard pattern (`agents/{any-coordinator}/status.json`). The roster's reads field uses yet another wildcard form (`agents/*/status.json`).\\n\\n**Impact:** Cosmetic. All paths are interpretable, but an automated routing parser would need to handle both patterns.\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-ROUTE-02: No Routing Dead-Ends\\nEvery coordinator chain eventually resolves to either `complete` or `blocked`, which the orchestrator handles. Every specialist resolves to `completed`, `passed`/`failed`, or `blocked`, all of which their parent coordinator handles. The orchestrator resolves to `delivered`, `delivered-with-gaps`, or `failed`, all of which the guide presents to the user. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-ROUTE-03: No Phantom Agent References\\nAll 67 agents referenced in routing tables exist in the roster and have corresponding `.agent.md` files. Verified by cross-referencing routing.json agent references against roster.json names and produced files. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-ROUTE-04: Child Result Code Completeness\\nSpot-checked 8 parent coordinators. Each handles all declared child result codes. For example, concept-coordinator handles concept-auditor's `passed`, `failed`, and `blocked`; worldbuilding-coordinator handles worldbuilding-auditor's `passed`, `failed`, and `blocked`. **PASS**\\n\\n### Manifest Hygiene\\n\\n#### ⚠️ AF-MANIFEST-01: Manifest Prepend Coverage\\n**FINDING:** 21 of 67 agents (orchestrator + 9 coordinators + 10 sub-coordinators + guide) do not mention `manifest.json` in their prompts. All 46 specialist agents include \\\"Prepend entry to `manifest.json`\\\" in their Status Contract sections.\\n\\n**Evidence:** `grep -L 'manifest.json'` returns the guide (exempt — no status.json), orchestrator, and all 19 coordinator/sub-coordinator agents. Since coordinators DO write status.json (their only write), they should also prepend to the manifest for audit trail completeness.\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-MANIFEST-02: Consistent Field Names\\nAll 46 manifest-aware agents use the same prepend instruction format. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-MANIFEST-03: Prepend Order\\nAll 46 manifest-aware agents specify \\\"Prepend entry to manifest.json\\\" which implies newest-first ordering. **PASS**\\n\\n### Artifact Ownership\\n\\n#### ⚠️ AF-ARTIFACT-01: Writer-Reader Completeness\\n**FINDING:** All 8 audit-report gate files (`audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json`) are written by auditors but have zero declared readers in roster.json. They ARE consumed — coordinator routing table actions reference them (e.g., \\\"read audit-reports/worldbuilding/gate.json to identify failing area\\\") — but they're not in any coordinator's `reads` array.\\n\\n**Evidence:** roster.json `reads` arrays for all 9 coordinators do not include `audit-reports/*/gate.json`. routing.json actions for 7 coordinators (worldbuilding, character, plotting, style, drafting, revision, beta-reading) reference these files. This means the roster's artifact contract is incomplete — it under-declares coordinator reads.\\n\\n#### ⚠️ AF-ARTIFACT-02: Multi-Writer Protocol\\n**FINDING:** `chapters/{N}/draft.md` has 4 writers (chapter-drafter, pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer) but no explicit read-modify-write protocol in the architecture artifacts section. The sequential dispatch order (enforced by sub-coordinator routing) prevents actual concurrent writes, but the artifact contract doesn't declare this ordering dependency.\\n\\n**Evidence:** architecture.json artifacts section includes ownership fields for tracking artifacts (continuity-tracker.json, foreshadowing-ledger.json, etc.) but not for `chapters/{N}/draft.md`. The creative-writing-coordinator's routing table enforces sequential dispatch (drafter → voice maintainer), and the quality-integration-coordinator enforces sequential dispatch (continuity-integrator → craft-enforcer), with the drafting-coordinator sequencing between the two sub-coordinators. The ordering is correct but implicit.\\n\\n#### ⚠️ AF-ARTIFACT-03: Cross-Cutting Tracker Write Conflicts\\n**FINDING:** 5 cross-cutting tracker artifacts are written by both the chapter-drafter (inline during drafting) and dedicated cross-cutting specialists (continuity-tracker, craft-tracker), without explicit merge-vs-overwrite protocol.\\n\\n**Evidence:** The chapter-drafter's Process Step 6 says \\\"Append to continuity-tracker.json\\\" and \\\"Update foreshadowing-ledger.json\\\" during chapter production. The continuity-tracker specialist's Process also writes to continuity-tracker.json and information-asymmetry-map.json. The craft-tracker writes to foreshadowing-ledger.json, mystery-box-inventory.json, emotional-throughline.json, and symbolic-motif-registry.json. These agents operate at different times (chapter-drafter during chapter production, cross-cutting specialists during post-drafting verification), but neither prompt specifies whether the later writer should merge or overwrite.\\n\\n#### ✅ AF-ARTIFACT-04: Create-Once Artifacts\\nSingle-writer artifacts (story-config.json, story-concept.json, craft-profile.json, style-guide.json, etc.) are each written by exactly one agent. **PASS**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Perspective 2: Fractal Workflow Evaluation\\n\\n### Pipeline Coherence\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-PIPELINE-01: Pass Ordering\\nThe 10 sequential creative phases (concept → worldbuilding → character → plotting → style → craft-tracker-init → drafting → continuity-verify → revision → beta-reading → polish → series-kb → delivery) are ordered correctly. Each phase's inputs come from completed earlier phases. No forward references. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-PIPELINE-02: Entry/Exit Conditions\\nEach coordinator has clear entry conditions (predecessor complete) and exit conditions (own auditor passed or blocked). The orchestrator's routing table enforces phase sequencing through status checks. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-PIPELINE-03: Re-Entry Safety\\nThe revision↔beta-reading feedback loop is bounded by `maxRevisionBetaCycles=2`. Per-chapter auditor retry loops are bounded by `maxAuditorRetries=3`. Both have forced-exit paths (write `blocked` at max retries). **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-PIPELINE-04: Pass Dependencies Match Data Flow\\narchitecture.json dataFlow section shows 27 artifact flows that match the phase ordering. No data flow entry references a downstream pass as an input source. **PASS**\\n\\n### Hierarchy Invariants\\n\\n#### ⚠️ FW-HIERARCHY-01: Orchestrator Dispatches Only Coordinators\\n**FINDING:** The orchestrator directly dispatches 3 specialist agents: continuity-tracker, series-kb-manager, and craft-tracker. These are cross-cutting concerns that span multiple coordinator domains.\\n\\n**Evidence:** roster.json shows romantic-fantasy-writer (orchestrator) has 12 children: 9 coordinators + 3 specialists. The orchestrator routing table includes direct dispatch rules for all 3: craft-tracker after style completes, continuity-tracker after drafting completes, series-kb-manager after polish completes.\\n\\n**Assessment:** This is a pragmatic exception. Cross-cutting specialists that serve the entire pipeline (continuity across all chapters, craft tracking across all chapters, series KB for the whole book) don't naturally belong to any single coordinator. Creating a cross-cutting-coordinator would add hierarchy depth without adding value. This exception should be documented as an explicit architectural decision.\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-HIERARCHY-02: Coordinators Dispatch Only Declared Children\\nAll 9 coordinators and 10 sub-coordinators dispatch only agents listed in their `children` array. Verified by cross-referencing routing table agent names against roster children lists. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-HIERARCHY-03: Specialists Never Dispatch\\nNo specialist prompt contains dispatch actions or routing tables. All 47 specialists write their own status.json and artifact outputs only. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-HIERARCHY-04: Depth-3 Structure\\nDepth-3 hierarchies are correctly implemented where specified. For example: worldbuilding-coordinator → physical-world-coordinator → geography-builder/culture-builder/history-builder. Depth-2 hierarchies (concept, style, revision, polish) correctly skip the sub-coordinator level. **PASS**\\n\\n### Convergence Logic\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-CONVERGE-01: Bounded Maximum\\nmaxAuditorRetries=3 (per-chapter per-coordinator), maxRevisionBetaCycles=2 (revision↔beta loop). Both defined in routing.json globalBounds. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-CONVERGE-02: Selective Re-Entry\\nOn auditor failure, coordinators reset only the specific sub-statuses needed for retry (e.g., concept-coordinator deletes concept-developer, craft-profile-selector, and concept-auditor statuses). The orchestrator's revision↔beta loop resets only revision and beta-reading statuses. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ℹ️ FW-CONVERGE-03: Bound Values Not Embedded\\n**FINDING:** Coordinator prompts reference `maxAuditorRetries` and `maxRevisionBetaCycles` as variables but don't specify their values or where to read them. The values (3 and 2 respectively) are defined only in routing.json globalBounds.\\n\\n**Evidence:** drafting-coordinator routing table: \\\"result == 'failed' AND chapterRetries < maxAuditorRetries\\\". The coordinator doesn't define maxAuditorRetries=3 anywhere in its prompt.\\n\\n**Impact:** Low — an LLM executing this prompt would need to find the value from the routing.json or pipeline config. Not a structural error, but a completeness gap in prompt self-containment.\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-CONVERGE-04: Forced Delivery Path\\nWhen maxAuditorRetries is reached, coordinators write `blocked`, which propagates to the orchestrator as a terminal failure. The orchestrator writes `failed` or `delivered-with-gaps` depending on the phase. **PASS**\\n\\n### Coordinator Purity\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-PURITY-01: Purity Rule Present\\nAll 9 coordinators, 10 sub-coordinators, and the orchestrator include a \\\"Pure Router Purity Rule\\\" section with the 5-point constraint list. **PASS**\\n\\n#### ℹ️ FW-PURITY-02: Orchestrator Purity Leak\\n**FINDING:** The orchestrator's beta-reading→revision feedback loop rule contains substantive conditional logic: \\\"check beta-synthesis verdicts: if any chapter verdict=='revision-required' AND revisionBetaCycles < maxRevisionBetaCycles → reset revision+beta status, re-dispatch revision-coordinator.\\\" This requires the orchestrator to interpret domain-specific artifact content (beta-synthesis verdicts per chapter), not just read status codes.\\n\\n**Evidence:** Rule 18 in the orchestrator routing table. Compare with all other rules which simply check `result == 'complete'` or `result == 'blocked'` — straightforward status code routing.\\n\\n**Impact:** Medium — this is the only purity violation in the entire system. It concentrates a non-trivial business decision (revision-required → re-enter revision) in the orchestrator rather than delegating to a specialist.\\n\\n### Anti-Laziness Enforcement\\n\\n#### ⚠️ FW-LAZY-01: Adversarial Agent Coverage\\n**FINDING:** Only 10 of ~16 adversarial/evaluation agents have Anti-Laziness Rules. Missing: beta-synthesizer (aggregation gate agent) and all 5 beta reader specialists (adversarial evaluation agents).\\n\\n**Evidence:** Agents with Anti-Laziness Rules: concept-auditor, worldbuilding-auditor, character-auditor, plotting-auditor, style-auditor, drafting-auditor, revision-auditor, beta-reading-auditor, craft-enforcer, craft-tracker. Agents WITHOUT Anti-Laziness Rules that perform adversarial/evaluative functions: beta-synthesizer (produces chapter verdicts that gate the revision loop), romance-beta-reader, fantasy-beta-reader, craft-beta-reader, sensitivity-beta-reader, originality-beta-reader (all produce adversarial feedback that gates chapter quality).\\n\\n#### ⚠️ FW-LAZY-02: Beta Reader Anti-Laziness Gap\\n**FINDING:** The 5 beta reader agents are structurally identical to auditors — they read chapter content and produce structured findings that influence quality gates — but they lack the same anti-laziness protections. A lazy beta reader that produces minimal feedback would cascade: the beta-synthesizer would see few findings → approve the chapter → skip revision → lower quality.\\n\\n**Evidence:** The beta-reading-auditor (which HAS anti-laziness rules) audits the synthesis output, but it can only catch issues if the beta readers actually reported them. The auditor can't independently re-read all chapters through all 5 lenses — it relies on the completeness of beta reader feedback.\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-LAZY-03: Evidence Requirements Present\\nAll 10 anti-laziness-equipped agents include explicit evidence requirements (\\\"quote the exact field value,\\\" \\\"provide specific evidence for every finding\\\"). **PASS**\\n\\n#### ✅ FW-LAZY-04: Zero-Findings Suspicion Clause\\nAll 10 anti-laziness-equipped agents include the zero-findings suspicion clause (\\\"If your first pass finds zero issues, do a second pass...\\\"). **PASS**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Comparison with Checklist-Validator\\n\\nThe checklist-validator found 40 warnings across 20 coordinator/orchestrator agents, all on the same two checks:\\n- V-SECTION-03: Missing `ask_questions` suppression in coordinators\\n- V-SECTION-04: Missing `## Key Invariants` section in coordinators\\n\\n**These are correctly categorized as low-severity** — coordinators are pure routers that don't perform substantive work, so ask_questions suppression and domain invariants are less critical for them.\\n\\n### New Issues Found by This Audit (not caught by checklist-validator)\\n\\n| ID | Severity | Category | Summary |\\n|----|----------|----------|---------|\\n| AF-CONTRACT-02 | warning | Dead result code | `revision-loop` declared but never emitted by 8 coordinators |\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-01 | warning | Incomplete reads | Audit-report gate files not in coordinator reads arrays |\\n| AF-MANIFEST-01 | warning | Manifest gaps | 21 agents missing manifest prepend instructions |\\n| AF-ROUTE-01 | info | Syntax inconsistency | Wildcard vs. specific paths in orchestrator routing |\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-02 | warning | Multi-writer protocol | chapters/{N}/draft.md has 4 writers without explicit ordering contract |\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-03 | warning | Write conflicts | Cross-cutting trackers have dual writers without merge protocol |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-01 | warning | Hierarchy exception | Orchestrator dispatches 3 specialists directly |\\n| FW-PURITY-01 | info | Purity leak | Orchestrator's beta-verdict rule contains domain logic |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-01 | info | Bound embedding | maxAuditorRetries not specified in coordinator prompts |\\n| FW-LAZY-01 | warning | Anti-laziness gaps | Beta-synthesizer lacks anti-laziness rules |\\n| FW-LAZY-02 | warning | Anti-laziness gaps | 5 beta readers lack anti-laziness rules |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Architecture Health Assessment\\n\\n**Overall: Healthy with addressable gaps.**\\n\\nThe system demonstrates strong architectural discipline:\\n- Pure router purity is maintained across all 20 coordinator/sub-coordinator agents (one minor orchestrator exception)\\n- Artifact data flows are acyclic at the phase level with clear producer-consumer relationships\\n- Convergence bounds exist for all iterative loops with forced-exit paths\\n- Anti-laziness rules are present on all phase-gate auditor agents\\n- The depth-3 hierarchy is correctly applied to complex subdomains (worldbuilding, character, plotting, drafting, beta-reading) and depth-2 to simpler ones (concept, style, revision, polish)\\n\\nThe gaps found are in **contract documentation** (incomplete reads/writes declarations, missing merge protocols, undeclared multi-writer ordering) and **adversarial coverage** (beta readers and synthesizer should have the same anti-laziness protections as auditors). These are prompt-level fixes (RE-003 re-entry category) — no roster or architectural changes needed.\\n\\n**Recommended gap-hunting priorities:**\\n1. Add anti-laziness rules to beta-synthesizer and all 5 beta readers (6 prompt edits)\\n2. Add audit-reports to coordinator reads arrays in roster.json (7 entries)\\n3. Add manifest.json prepend to coordinator/sub-coordinator prompts (20 prompt edits)\\n4. Clean up revision-loop dead code (8 roster entries + 1 routing rule)\\n5. Document multi-writer protocol for chapters/{N}/draft.md and cross-cutting trackers\\n6. Embed maxAuditorRetries/maxRevisionBetaCycles values in coordinator prompts\\n\"}"
            }
