-profile-selector as the writer, and there's no plot-architect agent in the roster at all. This suggests the architecture file might be outdated or the role names have shifted during implementation.\n\nNow I need to systematically map every artifact from the architecture against what's actually implemented in the roster to identify any orphaned artifacts or missing writers and readers. I should also verify that schema files exist for all artifacts and check on those cross-cutting tracker files like the foreshadowing ledger and mystery box inventory.\n\nLooking at the schema coverage, I'm seeing schemas for agent status, audit reports, beta feedback, chapter outlines, chapters, characters, craft profile, and cross-cutting trackers. Let me continue checking what other schemas are defined and whether they cover all the artifacts referenced in the architecture. I'm seeing some significant misalignments between what architecture.json specifies and what the actual roster contains. The craft-profile writer is named differently than expected, the world-bible artifacts are assigned to a generic worldbuilder instead of specialized builders like geography and culture experts, and the character-related assignments don't match the more granular roles that actually exist in the roster—same issue with plot structure where the architecture references agents that don't match the real implementation. The naming mismatches between architecture.json and roster.json are actually a cross-reference integrity issue rather than an artifact coverage problem — the roster is the ground truth with proper read/write mappings, while architecture.json is just a design document with collapsed role names. What I really need to verify for Category 4 is whether every artifact has at least one actual writer and reader from the roster. Now I'm moving on to test coverage, where I've identified 21 test scenarios organized by category: happy-path, auditor-rejection, and convergence-exhaustion each have two