overed | TEST-001 (single chapter), TEST-002 (multi-chapter) |\\n| Auditor rejection | ⚠ Partial | Concept (TEST-003), Drafting (TEST-004). Missing: plotting, character, style, worldbuilding, revision, beta-reading |\\n| Convergence exhaustion | ✓ Covered | Worldbuilding (TEST-005), Revision-beta (TEST-006) |\\n| Re-entry | ✓ Covered | TEST-007 |\\n| Per-chapter iteration | ✓ Covered | TEST-008, TEST-009 |\\n| Cross-cutting agents | ✓ Covered | TEST-010, TEST-011, TEST-012 |\\n| Edge cases | ✓ Covered | TEST-013, TEST-014, TEST-015 |\\n| Coordinator routing | ✓ Covered | TEST-018, TEST-019 |\\n| Revision-beta loop | ✓ Covered | TEST-020, TEST-021 |\\n| Forced delivery | ✗ Missing | No test for delivered-with-gaps |\\n| Series/sequel | ✗ Missing | No test for sequel production |\\n| Parallel dispatch | ✗ Missing | No test for beta-reader fan-out |\\n\\n### Findings\\n\\n#### TC-001 (warning): No test for delivered-with-gaps\\nThe orchestrator has 3 result codes: `delivered`, `delivered-with-gaps`, `failed`. The routing table defines two paths to `delivered-with-gaps` (polish-coordinator blocked, series-kb-manager blocked), but no test exercises either. The string \\\"delivered-with-gaps\\\" appears nowhere in test-plan.json.\\n\\n#### TC-002 (warning): 3 missing golden test fixture directories\\n- TEST-014 (P2): \\\"Edge case no style references\\\" — defined in plan but no test fixtures\\n- TEST-016 (P2): \\\"Specialist geography-builder creates map\\\" — defined in plan but no test fixtures\\n- TEST-017 (P2): \\\"Specialist romance-arc-designer dual arc\\\" — defined in plan but no test fixtures\\n\\nAll 3 are P2 priority. 18 of 21 scenarios have golden test directories with context.json, expected-status.json, and README.md.\\n\\n#### TC-003 (warning): No plotting-auditor rejection test\\nConcept-auditor rejection is tested (TEST-003), drafting-auditor rejection is tested (TEST-004), but plotting-auditor rejection is not. The plotting phase has the most complex sub-coordinator tree (structural-design + chapter-design) and its retry path involves deleting sub-coordinator + specialist + auditor statuses across multiple agents.\\n\\n#### TC-004 (warning): No character-auditor rejection test\\nSimilar to plotting, the character phase has depth-3 hierarchy (character-coordinator → core-characters + ensemble → 4 specialists). The auditor-failed recovery deletes sub-coordinator + specialist + auditor statuses and re-dispatches. This complex recovery path is untested.\\n\\n#### TC-005 (warning): No series/sequel test\\nThe domain brief states \\\"Designed for series production from day one\\\" and the architecture includes series-kb-manager, sequelOf/seriesId fields in story-config, and series-kb/index.json. No test scenario exercises sequel creation where a previous book's knowledge base influences the current book.\\n\\n#### TC-006 (warning): No parallel dispatch test\\nBeta-reading uses parallel fan-out: genre-lens-coordinator dispatches 2 readers in parallel, craft-lens-coordinator dispatches 3 readers in parallel. No test verifies fan-out/fan-in synchronization or partial failure within a parallel group.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Category 6: Cross-Reference Integrity\\n\\n**Methodology**: Verified 7 cross-reference dimensions:\\n1. Routing.json agent name references → roster.json (regex extraction from all dispatchOrder, routingTable actions, parallelGroups)\\n2. Roster agent names → prompt files in produced-output/agents/\\n3. Prompt files → roster (check for orphans)\\n4. Coordinator/orchestrator/sub-coordinator roster entries → routing.json entries\\n5. Result codes in routing conditions → roster resultCodes arrays\\n6. Roster parent/children bidirectional consistency\\n7. Architecture.json artifact writer/reader names → roster agent names\\n\\n**Items checked**: 67 roster agents, 21 routing entries, 67 prompt files, 42 artifact metadata entries\\n\\n### Integrity Matrix\\n\\n| Check | Result | Details |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Routing → Roster names | ✓ Clean | All agent references in routing actions resolve |\\n| Roster → Prompt files | ✓ Clean | 67/67 agents have .agent.md files |\\n| Prompt files → Roster | ✓ Clean | 0 orphan prompt files |\\n| Coordinators → Routing entries | ✓ Clean | All 21 coordinators/orchestrator have routing entries |\\n| Result codes in routing | ✓ Clean | All condition result codes match roster resultCodes |\\n| Parent/child integrity | ✓ Clean | All references bidirectionally valid |\\n| Architecture → Roster names | ✗ Dirty | 6 ghost agent names |\\n| Roster children ↔ Dispatch order | ✓ Clean | Children and dispatch lists match |\\n| Global bounds consistency | ✓ Clean | test-plan.json and routing.json bounds match |\\n\\n### Findings\\n\\n#### CR-001 (warning): 6 ghost agent names in architecture.json\\nSame 6 ghost agents detailed in AC-003. The architecture.json artifact metadata uses pre-depth-analysis names that were split or renamed during depth analysis. The routing.json and roster.json are fully consistent with each other and with the prompt files — this is isolated document drift in architecture.json only.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Second-Pass Verification\\n\\nPer anti-laziness rules, I performed a second verification pass:\\n\\n1. **Re-checked Category 4**: Confirmed all 42 artifacts have at least one real writer AND reader in roster.json. The style-analyzer writes only status.json (no separate analysis artifact) — verified this is correct because the coordinator passes analysis context in dispatch (agent-as-function pattern).\\n2. **Re-checked Category 5**: Searched for alternative keyword matches (e.g., \\\"plot\\\" instead of \\\"plotting\\\") — no additional coverage found for the identified gaps.\\n3. **Re-checked Category 6**: Verified that routing table prose-format actions (which contain agent names embedded in sentences) were correctly parsed by regex — all `romantic-fantasy-writer-*` names extracted and validated.\\n\\nSecond pass confirmed all findings. 