        "reasoning_text": "All three specialists have completed:\n- Coverage hunter: dirty (4 gaps: 3 critical, 1 warning)\n- Artifact hunter: dirty (10 gaps: 0 critical, 10 warnings)\n- Infrastructure hunter: dirty (8 gaps: 0 critical, 8 warnings)\n\nNow I need to aggregate. Let me compute:\n- totalCategories = 9\n- categoriesClean: Count categories with zero gaps\n  - Cat 1 (Subdomain Coverage): clean (0 gaps)\n  - Cat 2 (Invariant Enforcement): dirty (2 gaps)\n  - Cat 3 (Routing Completeness): dirty (2 gaps)\n  - Cat 4 (Artifact Coverage): dirty (3 gaps)\n  - Cat 5 (Test Coverage): dirty (6 gaps)\n  - Cat 6 (Cross-Reference Integrity): dirty (1 gap)\n  - Cat 7 (Bootstrap Completeness): marked clean but actually has 1 gap, which seems inconsistent\n\nI'm noticing the infrastructure hunter flagged category 7 as clean despite it containing a gap. For my aggregation, I'll stick with the gap count as the source of truth—zero gaps means clean, anything else is dirty. So only category 1 comes out clean, leaving 8 categories with gaps. Tallying everything up, I'm getting 22 total gaps with 3 critical ones.\n\nNow I need to find the earliest re-entry target across all the gaps. Looking at what each hunter identified, the coverage hunter points to execution phases while the artifact and infrastructure hunters mostly reference planning phases. Planning appears earliest, so that's my suggested re-entry point.\n\nI'm now preparing to write out the gap details and update the manifest files accordingly.",
